# Line endings. TASKS.md Part 11 left this open: "core.autocrlf=true with no
# .gitattributes means a fresh clone checks out CRLF - and line endings measurably
# change the emitted HTML here. The fix is `* text=auto eol=lf`; it was not added
# because it changes checkout behaviour for everyone."
#
# It is added now, and this is the measurement that decided it.
#
# `git ls-files --eol` over the whole repo, 22 Sep 2026:
#
#     202  i/lf    w/lf
#      53  i/lf    w/crlf
#      43  i/-text w/-text
#       5  i/none  w/none     (five empty .svg left over from create-next-app)
#
# EVERY text file is ALREADY LF IN THE INDEX. That is the fact that makes this
# file safe: `text=auto eol=lf` renormalises nothing in history and produces no
# content diff. It changes only what a checkout writes, which is exactly the
# defect. The 53 CRLF files are CRLF because this machine has core.autocrlf=true
# and nothing overrode it; the same commit checked out on the Linux CI agent gets
# LF for all 53. The emitted HTML is therefore a function of who cloned the repo.
#
# The 53, for the record: README.md, design/canvas.json, the ten files in
# helloverify-web/design-src/artboards/, src/components/sections/Packages.tsx, and
# 40 files under tools/port/ (six .py and 34 jsx/*.txt).
#
# WHY NO SHELL CHECK EVER CAUGHT THIS, which is the part worth carrying forward:
# on src/components/sections/Packages.tsx - the single CRLF file in src/ - Git Bash
# `grep -c $'\r'` and `cat -A` both report ZERO carriage returns. Only
# `git ls-files --eol` and Python opened with newline="" can see them. Every
# EOL assertion in this repo is shell-based and is therefore blind to this class
# of problem (TASKS.md "Demo readiness", defect 3).
#
# REJECTED: fix Packages.tsx by hand and skip this file. It corrects one file on
# one machine and leaves the next clone free to reintroduce all 53.
#
# WHAT A DEVELOPER SEES after this lands: nothing, until they next check a file
# out. `git add --renormalize .` is a no-op here because the index is already LF;
# the 52 files that are CRLF on disk (all but the artboards, pinned below) stay
# CRLF on disk until a fresh clone or an explicit re-checkout writes them again.
# They do NOT appear as modified in the meantime, because with `text` set Git
# normalises CRLF to LF before comparing against the index.
#
# Measured, not assumed: `git status --short` was captured with this file present
# and again with it renamed away, and the two outputs differ by exactly one line
# - the name of this file in the untracked list. Zero working-tree diffs.

# The default for everything Git decides is text. eol=lf rather than plain
# `text=auto` because `text=auto` alone defers to core.autocrlf, which is the
# setting that produced the mess above.
* text=auto eol=lf

# ── Never normalise these ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `text=auto` already sniffs binaries by content, and it got all 43 right here.
# They are named anyway: a blanket CRLF rewrite over src/ has already corrupted
# src/app/favicon.ico and the four .woff2 faces once, and content-sniffing is
# the kind of heuristic that is right until the day a font begins with printable
# bytes. The subset faces in src/fonts/ are build inputs for §9.2's font
# pipeline; a single flipped byte in one is a broken glyph on every page.
#
# `binary` is the macro for `-text -diff`, and it WINS over the `* ... eol=lf`
# above even though `git check-attr eol` still answers "lf" for these paths: the
# eol attribute is only consulted when `text` is set. Checked rather than
# trusted - `git ls-files --eol` reports
#     i/-text w/-text attr/-text    helloverify-web/src/app/favicon.ico
#     i/-text w/-text attr/-text    helloverify-web/src/fonts/newsreader-roman.woff2
# and `attr/-text` is Git saying it will not convert them.
*.ico   binary
*.woff2 binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.webp  binary
*.avif  binary
*.pdf   binary
*.xlsx  binary
*.pyc   binary

# ── The design-canvas capture, which is legitimately CRLF ────────────────────
# helloverify-web/design-src/artboards/ is a verbatim export from the design
# canvas, and it is a live build input rather than reference material:
# tools/a11y/check-css-color.mjs and tools/a11y/check-contrast.mjs read it (both
# are in `npm run check:all`), and tools/port/{extract,build-css,build-sections,
# logical-css}.py open it directly.
#
# Its CRLF pair counts are recorded per board in TASKS.md - 609/603/541/580/509/
# 440/482/503/453 - and were used as the byte-level evidence that Part 2a's
# colour-token edit changed one occurrence per file and nothing else. Under
# `eol=lf` those numbers would be zero on a Linux agent and nine non-zero here,
# which is the same non-determinism this file exists to remove, pointed the other
# way. `eol=crlf` makes the recorded state true on every platform including CI.
#
# Note this is the CURRENT state of the checkout, not a change: these ten files
# are already i/lf w/crlf. The index stays LF; only the checkout is pinned.
#
# design/ at the repo root is the same kind of capture but is read by nothing
# (`grep -rn` over tools/ finds zero references), so it takes the LF default -
# which also settles the one file there that is inconsistent today,
# design/canvas.json, CRLF beside nine LF siblings.
helloverify-web/design-src/artboards/** text eol=crlf

# ── Files whose EOL is not ours to choose ────────────────────────────────────
# A .sh or a Git hook with CRLF fails on Linux with "bad interpreter: no such
# file or directory", which is a confusing way to learn about line endings. The
# `*` rule above already covers them; they are pinned explicitly because
# tools/git-hooks/pre-commit is the one file in this repo whose breakage would be
# silent in the direction that matters - a hook Git cannot execute does not warn,
# it simply does not scan.
*.sh                                text eol=lf
helloverify-web/tools/git-hooks/*   text eol=lf

# ── Diff behaviour ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Lockfile conflicts are resolved by re-running `npm install`, never by hand-
# merging 445,000 lines. `-diff` keeps it out of review diffs; it stays fully
# versioned.
helloverify-web/package-lock.json   -diff linguist-generated=true
